= Solution
{parent=a}

Direct <matrix multiplication> gives $P^2=P-I$ and hence $P^3=-I$. It follows that
$$
\begin{aligned}
P^0&=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},&P&=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},&P^2&=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&-1\end{pmatrix},\\
P^3&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},&P^4&=\begin{pmatrix}-1&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},&P^5&=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},
\end{aligned}
$$
and $P^6=I$. These six matrices are distinct; consequently no smaller positive power is the <identity element>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\langle P\rangle=\{I,P,P^2,P^3,P^4,P^5\}\cong C_6,\qquad\operatorname{ord}(P)=6.}
$$
Products add exponents modulo six and $P^{-1}=P^5$. This verifies the <cyclic group> structure explicitly.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Observe that $A=-I$, $B^2=I$ and $C=-B$. Thus $A^2=B^2=C^2=I$, while $AB=BA=C$, $AC=CA=B$ and $BC=CB=A$. The <multiplication table>, with the left factor labeling the row, is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|cccc}
&I&A&B&C\\\hline
I&I&A&B&C\\
A&A&I&C&B\\
B&B&C&I&A\\
C&C&B&A&I
\end{array}.}
$$
It proves <closure> of the set. <Associativity> is inherited from <matrix multiplication>, $I$ is the <identity element>, and every element is its own <inverse element>. Therefore this set is a <group>. The table is symmetric, so it is an <Abelian group>; its three nonidentity elements have order two, identifying it as the <Klein four-group> $C_2\times C_2$.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $T$ be the set of <torsion elements> of the given <Abelian group>. It contains the <identity element>, which has order one. If $g,h\in T$ have orders $m,n$, set $\ell=\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)$. Commutativity permits powers of the product to split:
$$
(gh^{-1})^\ell=g^\ell h^{-\ell}=e.
$$
Hence $gh^{-1}$ has finite order, dividing $\ell$, and lies in $T$. The nonempty set $T$ therefore satisfies the <subgroup test> and is a <subgroup>:
$$
\boxed{T=\{g\in G:\operatorname{ord}(g)<\infty\}\leq G.}
$$
This is the <torsion subgroup>. The proof uses commutativity exactly when rearranging the factors in $(gh^{-1})^\ell$; one cannot use that step in an arbitrary noncommutative <group>. The subgroup need not itself be a finite set.

