= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $A(a,b,c)$ with $a$ in entry $(1,2)$, $b$ in entry $(1,3)$ and $c$ in entry $(2,3)$, and perform all arithmetic in the <finite field> $\mathbb F_5$. Direct multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{A(a,b,c)A(a',b',c')=A(a+a',\ b+b'+ac',\ c+c').}
$$
Thus the set is closed. <Associativity> comes from <matrix multiplication>. The <identity element> is $A(0,0,0)$, and solving the product formula for it gives
$$
\boxed{A(a,b,c)^{-1}=A(-a,\ ac-b,\ -c).}
$$
Both orders of multiplication with this inverse give the identity. Hence these matrices form a <finite group>, an instance of the <Heisenberg group over a prime field>. Each coordinate has five independent possibilities, with distinct triples giving distinct matrices, so
$$
\boxed{|G|=5^3=125.}
$$
The explicit coordinate convention matters because the central coordinate is $b$ here; other descriptions of the <Heisenberg group over a prime field> may place the central coordinate last.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A member of $H$ is $A(t,b,t)$. The multiplication law from part (a) becomes
$$
A(t,b,t)A(s,d,s)=A(t+s,\ b+d+ts,\ t+s).
$$
It stays in $H$ and is unchanged when the two factors are exchanged, since $ts=st$. The identity lies in $H$, and $A(t,b,t)^{-1}=A(-t,t^2-b,-t)$ also lies in $H$. Thus $H$ is an <Abelian subgroup>, namely the <Abelian plane subgroup of a finite Heisenberg group> in this coordinate convention.

There are five choices each for $t,b$, so
$$
\boxed{|H|=25,\qquad [G:H]=\frac{125}{25}=5.}
$$
This verifies <Lagrange's theorem>: the subgroup order divides the group order, and the number of distinct left <cosets> is five.

Explicitly, the map $\varphi(A(a,b,c))=a-c$ is a surjective <group homomorphism> to the additive group of $\mathbb F_5$, by the product formula. Its <kernel of a group homomorphism> is $H$. Thus $H$ is even a <normal subgroup>; the five left <cosets> are the level sets $a-c=d$ for $d=0,1,2,3,4$, with representatives $A(d,0,0)$. Each contains 25 elements, providing a direct count as well.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Squaring with the product law gives
$$
A(a,b,c)^2=A(2a,\ 2b+ac,\ 2c).
$$
Equality to the <identity matrix> requires $2a=2c=0$ in $\mathbb F_5$. Since 2 has a multiplicative inverse modulo 5, this forces $a=c=0$. The middle condition then gives $2b=0$, hence $b=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\{A\in G:A^2=I\}=\{I\}.}
$$
This agrees with <Lagrange's theorem>: a group of odd order has no element of order two.

The subset specified by $b\ne0$ **is not a subgroup**. It omits the <identity element>. It also fails <closure>: both $A(0,1,0)$ and $A(0,4,0)$ lie in it, but their product is $A(0,0,0)=I$, whose $b$ coordinate is zero.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
