= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A finite-dimensional <representation> of a <group> $G$ on a vector space $V$ is a <group homomorphism> $D:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V)$. Thus each $D(X)$ is invertible and
$$
D(XY)=D(X)D(Y),\qquad D(I)=I_V.
$$
Here the group identity and the identity linear map are different objects, represented by corresponding symbols. Applying the multiplication property to $XX^{-1}=X^{-1}X=I$ gives
$$
D(X)D(X^{-1})=I_V=D(X^{-1})D(X).
$$
By the defining property of a <matrix inverse>,
$$
\boxed{D(X^{-1})=D(X)^{-1}.}
$$
If the definition only states the multiplication law together with invertibility, $D(I)^2=D(I)$ already forces $D(I)=I_V$ after multiplication by its inverse.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the identity relation as printed in the PDF, $XYZ=I$. Since $Z^2=I$, multiplying on the right by $Z$ gives $XY=Z$. Also
$$
YX=(XY)^{-1}=Z^{-1}=Z=XY,
$$
because $X,Y,Z$ are involutions. Thus $X$ and $Y$ commute, and so does their product $Z$ with either of them. Therefore **the group is Abelian**, and is the <Klein four-group>.

Each <conjugacy class> is a singleton. Over the complex numbers every <irreducible representation of a finite abelian group> is one-dimensional. Choose independent signs $\varepsilon=\chi(X)$ and $\eta=\chi(Y)$; the relations force $\varepsilon^2=\eta^2=1$ and $\chi(Z)=\varepsilon\eta$. Hence the complete <character table> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
&I&X&Y&Z\\\hline
\chi_{++}&1&1&1&1\\
\chi_{+-}&1&1&-1&-1\\
\chi_{-+}&1&-1&1&-1\\
\chi_{--}&1&-1&-1&1
\end{array}.}
$$
These four distinct <one-dimensional representations> exhaust the irreducible possibilities: there are four <conjugacy classes>, and their squared dimensions already sum to $|G|=4$. The rows also satisfy <character orthogonality>. The local transcription damages the final symbol in the defining relation; the PDF makes the identity $I$ unambiguous.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $Y_p=D(Y)$. Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
Y_p^2=\begin{pmatrix}1&p-p\\0&1\end{pmatrix}=I_2
$$
for every real $p$. Since $D(X)=-I_2$ commutes with all matrices and $D(Z)=-Y_p$, the remaining relations also hold:
$$
D(X)^2=D(Z)^2=I_2,\qquad D(X)D(Y)=D(Z),\qquad D(X)D(Y)D(Z)=I_2.
$$
All matrices are invertible, and the relations reproduce the <multiplication table> of the <Klein four-group>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p\in\mathbb R\text{ is arbitrary}.}
$$
A <representation> need not be an <orthogonal representation>; imposing orthogonality on the displayed matrices would be an extra condition.

The <characters> are their traces, independent of $p$:
$$
\boxed{\big(\chi(I),\chi(X),\chi(Y),\chi(Z)\big)=(2,-2,0,0).}
$$
In terms of part (b), $\chi=\chi_{-+}+\chi_{--}$, so the representation is reducible. For an explicit <change of basis>, take
$$
S=\begin{pmatrix}1&-p/2\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Then $S^{-1}D(Y)S=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$ and $S^{-1}D(Z)S=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, while $D(X)$ remains $-I_2$. These are the <equivalent sheared representations of the Klein four-group>; the parameter changes the basis, not the two irreducible constituents.
